**`/` (root)** — top of the entire filesystem; everything lives under here.

**Common directories:**
- `/bin` — essential commands like `ls`, `cp`, `mv` available to all users
- `/etc` — system-wide config files (network, users, services)
- `/home` — personal directories for each user (`/home/alice`, etc.)
- `/var` — variable data that grows over time: logs, databases, mail
- `/tmp` — temporary files, cleared on reboot
- `/usr` — most user-installed programs and libraries (`/usr/bin`, `/usr/lib`)
- `/dev` — device files representing hardware (disks, terminals, USB)

**System/virtual directories:**
- `/proc` — virtual filesystem exposing kernel and process info (not real files)
- `/sys` — hardware and driver info exposed by the kernel
- `/boot` — bootloader, kernel image, initial ramdisk
- `/sbin` — admin-only binaries (`fdisk`, `iptables`)
- `/lib` — shared libraries needed by `/bin` and `/sbin`
- `/mnt` / `/opt` — mount points for external filesystems / optional third-party software




